  doi = {10.1109/TASC.2019.2892584},
  url = {http://arxiv.org/abs/1901.03819},
  urldate = {2023-10-18},
  abstract = {Nb{$<$}inline-formula xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"{$><$}tex-math notation="LaTeX"{$>\$\_$}\{3\}\${$<$}/tex-math{$><$}/inline-formula{$>$}Sn~is a superconductor of great relevance for perspective radiofrequency (RF) applications. We present for the first time surface impedance ({$<$}inline-formula xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"{$><$}tex-math notation="LaTeX"{$>\$$}Z\_s\${$<$}/tex-math{$><$}/inline-formula{$>$}) measurements at 15~GHz and low-RF field amplitude on Nb{$<$}inline-formula xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"{$><$}tex-math notation="LaTeX"{$>\$\_$}\{3\}\${$<$}/tex-math{$><$}/inline-formula{$>$}Sn~in high magnetic fields up to 12~T, with the aim of increasing the knowledge of Nb{$<$}inline-formula xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"{$><$}tex-math notation="LaTeX"{$>\$\_$}\{3\}\${$<$}/tex-math{$><$}/inline-formula{$>$}Sn~behavior in such conditions. {$<$}inline-formula xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"{$><$}tex-math notation="LaTeX"{$>\$$}Z\_s\${$<$}/tex-math{$><$}/inline-formula{$>$}is a fundamental material parameter that directly gives useful information about the dissipative and reactive phenomena when the superconductor is subjected to high-frequency excitations. Therefore, we present an analysis of the measured {$<$}inline-formula xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"{$><$}tex-math notation="LaTeX"{$>\$$}Z\_s\${$<$}/tex-math{$><$}/inline-formula{$>$}with the aim of extracting interesting data about pinning in Nb{$<$}inline-formula xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"{$><$}tex-math notation="LaTeX"{$>\$\_$}\{3\}\${$<$}/tex-math{$><$}/inline-formula{$>$}Sn~ at high frequencies. From {$<$}inline-formula xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"{$><$}tex-math notation="LaTeX"{$>\$$}Z\_s\${$<$}/tex-math{$><$}/inline-formula{$>$}, we extract the vortex motion complex resistivity to obtain the {$<$}inline-formula xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"{$><$}tex-math notation="LaTeX"{$>\$$}r\${$<$}/tex-math{$><$}/inline-formula{$>$}-parameter and the depinning frequency {$<$}inline-formula xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"{$><$}tex-math notation="LaTeX"{$>\$\backslash$}nu \_p\${$<$}/tex-math{$><$}/inline-formula{$>$}in high magnetic fields. The comparison of the results with the literature shows that the measured {$<$}inline-formula xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"{$><$}tex-math notation="LaTeX"{$>\$\backslash$}nu \_p\${$<$}/tex-math{$><$}/inline-formula{$>$}on bulk Nb{$<$}inline-formula xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"{$><$}tex-math notation="LaTeX"{$>\$\_$}\{3\}\${$<$}/tex-math{$><$}/inline-formula{$>$}Sn~is several times greater than that of pure Nb. This demonstrates how Nb{$<$}inline-formula xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"{$><$}tex-math notation="LaTeX"{$>\$\_$}\{3\}\${$<$}/tex-math{$><$}/inline-formula{$>$}Sn~can be a good candidate for RF technological applications, also in high magnetic fields.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {Condensed Matter - Superconductivity},
  annotation = {0 citations (INSPIRE 2023/10/18)\\
